afk: named eval kernels run in parallel; one cell per kernel at a time.

Why: one eval call at a time per agent was the only way to use the kernel.
A cell auto-backgrounded after 10 s keeps running, and the next call on the
same kernel queued behind it inside the runner while its own timeout was
already ticking; when that timeout fired, the process-wide SIGINT landed in
the OTHER (running) cell, and an unresponsive cell got the kernel killed
(2026-10-04 nmm session: a `time.sleep(720)` cell followed by status checks).

What it does:
- eval gains an optional `kernel` param (1-32 of [A-Za-z0-9_-]). A name
  selects its own retained Python/JS kernel (session id suffix
  `:kernel=<name>`), with its own state and process; omitted = the default
  kernel, unchanged.
- The tool's concurrency is per call: named-kernel calls are "shared" (run
  alongside each other in one turn), default-kernel calls stay "exclusive".
- A host-side lane per (backend, cwd, kernel session id) runs one cell at a
  time and arms the cell's timeout only once it holds the lane, so waiting
  never counts against a cell and its timeout can never interrupt another
  cell. A caller abort while waiting just drops out of the queue.
- Named-kernel calls skip speculative shadow execution.
- eval.md documents kernels and the one-cell-per-kernel rule.

What must survive a rebase:
- backend.execute receives `kernel`; resolvePythonKernelIdentity /
  resolveJsKernelIdentity take an optional kernel and suffix the session
  id via namedKernelSessionId(). Kernel-defined tools (task/eval-tools.ts)
  still resolve the default kernel.
- acquireKernelLane wraps exactly backend.execute; idle timer created after.
- test/tools/eval-named-kernels.test.ts: the lane test fails without the
  lane (first cell gets KeyboardInterrupt).

Rebased 18.2.10 -> 18.6.0: upstream has no named/parallel kernels of its
own; eval stays exclusive with one kernel per session. Upstream rewrote
eval.md (terse; now says "Subagents have separate kernels" itself, so this
patch no longer depends on omp-task-private-eval-kernel, which was dropped)
and shortened the eval schema field descriptions; the kernel doc line and
the `kernel?` description were re-cut in that style. py/index.ts now reads
settings via cfgPython*; the kernel param was re-threaded there. Lane,
concurrency, shadow-discard and execute hunks only moved.

